# 2. Data and Windowing

## 2.1 Data set

We analyze consecutive prime gaps in the interval
\[
[10^{10},\,3\times 10^{10}).
\]
Let \(p_n\) denote the \(n\)-th prime. The data set contains
\[
n = 844{,}953{,}415
\]
consecutive prime gaps
\[
g_n = p_{n+1}-p_n.
\]
The center of the interval in log scale is
\[
\log p_{\text{center}} = 23.575157.
\]
The raw gap variance is
\[
\operatorname{Var}(g) = 438.670412,
\]
with standard deviation
\[
\operatorname{sd}(g) = 20.944.
\]
The lag-one covariance is
\[
\operatorname{Cov} = -11.001171,
\]
and the raw lag-one correlation is
\[
\rho_1^{\text{raw}} = -0.025078.
\]
These values reproduce the frozen predictions made before the \(10^{10}\) run.

## 2.2 Local detrending

We follow the windowed local detrending procedure frozen in the analysis protocol and used in Berramdane (2026a). The detrending removes the local scale trend within each window so that the remaining signal reflects serial dependence rather than slow variation in gap size. No parameter of the detrending is changed in the present study. The residual gaps are the input to the covariance and correlation analyses reported below.

## 2.3 Phase conditioning

For each primorial modulus
\[
M \in \{6,\,30,\,210,\,2310,\,30030,\,510510,\,9699690\},
\]
we condition on the opening-prime residue
\[
r = p_n \bmod M.
\]
This partitions the gaps into \(\varphi(M)\) phase classes. Table 1 lists, for each \(M\), the average number of gaps per class \(n_c\) and the minimum class count \(\min\_class\).

**Table 1. Phase class sizes.**

| \(M\) | \(n_c\) | \(\min\_class\) |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 6 | 422,476,707.50 | 422,475,051 |
| 30 | 105,619,176.88 | 105,617,814 |
| 210 | 17,603,196.15 | 17,601,182 |
| 2310 | 1,760,319.61 | 1,758,565 |
| 30030 | 146,693.30 | 146,003 |
| 510510 | 9,168.33 | 8,936 |
| 9699690 | 509.35 | 433 |

The smallest class count occurs at \(M=9699690\), where \(\min\_class = 433\). All classes are non-empty and sufficiently populated for the covariance decomposition and the bootstrap analyses reported in the following sections.

## 2.4 Windowing and resampling

The \(10^{10}\) data set is divided into 40 contiguous blocks for the block bootstrap. Confidence intervals for \(\rho_{\text{resid}}\) are obtained by resampling these blocks. The bootstrap uses independent seeds for each \(M\) and is described in the frozen protocol. The resulting 95% intervals are reported in Section 5.

## 2.5 Reproducibility

All results in this paper are computed from the frozen \(10^{10}\) data set. No value is reported without a corresponding run. The file `phase_decomposition_v2.csv` contains the full table of \(M\), the covariance decomposition, the variance split, and the bootstrap intervals.
